  "governed_construct": "Each named function in the file: a function declaration (including async and generator declarations), a function or arrow-function expression that initialises a const/let/var binding (the binding name is the function's name), a class method, and an object-literal method or property whose value is a function. Constructors, getters/setters, and anonymous inline callbacks (e.g. arguments to .map/.reduce) are not instances. Code inside comments and string/template literals is not code and is not an instance.",
  "interpretation": "The rule asks that every function's name begin with a verb. The name is split at its first camelCase/PascalCase/underscore word boundary and the first word must be a verb (get, set, is, has, fetch, load, save, create, compute, calculate, validate, render, handle, add, apply, send, parse, generate, write, print, format, stream, etc.; boolean-predicate prefixes is/has/can/should count as verbs). A name whose first word is a noun or adjective (userProfile, priceSummary, cartItems, discountRate, userSettings) violates. A name that merely starts with the letters of a verb but whose first word is a noun (getter, issue, settings) is not verb-initial. Constructors and accessors are excluded because their names are fixed by the language or denote properties; anonymous callbacks have no name and are excluded. The verdict is SATISFIED when the file has at least one governed function and all are verb-initial, VIOLATED when any governed function is not, and NOT_APPLICABLE when the file defines no governed function (types, interfaces and data constants only). Function-like text appearing only inside comments or string/template literals is ignored.",
